@page "/form"

<CatHead Title="Form layout">
    <code>.form-field</code> stacks a label, a control and a hint; a <a href="/grid"><code>.sedna-grid</code></a> puts two
    side by side; <code>.form-section</code> groups them and <code>.form-actions</code> closes the
    form. The controls themselves each have their own page under <strong>Inputs</strong>.
</CatHead>

<CatGroup Title="Fields and rows">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.Fields" Title="Fields" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>Label, control, hint. The hint explains the consequence, not the control.</p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.TwoUpRows" Title="Two-up rows" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>.sedna-grid sedna-grid--sm sedna-grid--fit</code> lays fields out side by side and collapses to one column when there
            is not room for two &mdash; no breakpoint to pick.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.FullRow" Title="Full-row fields" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>.sedna-span-full</code> on a child of that grid — the long field
            among the short ones: a description, a Markdown editor, a checkbox line, a hint that belongs
            to the row rather than to one field. It works in
            <a href="/grid">any grid</a>, down to a single-column phone, so there is no breakpoint and no
            <code>style="grid-column:1 / -1"</code> in the markup.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Sections and actions">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.SectionsActions" Title="Fieldset sections" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>.form-section</code> is a <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code> with the browser's border and
            inset legend removed. Use the real element &mdash; the legend is announced with every control
            in the group, which a heading above a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> is not &mdash; and put the
            fields in <code>.form-section-body</code>, because a <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code> is not a flex
            item and lands somewhere different in every engine if the fieldset is the flex container.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.SectionTitle" Title="Sections without a fieldset" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>.form-section-title</code> on a plain heading, for a region rather than a set of related
            controls. Same rule, without claiming a grouping that is not there &mdash; a
            <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code> tells a screen reader these answers belong together.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.ActionsAlignment" Title="Action placement" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>.form-actions</code> wraps instead of overflowing, so three buttons on a phone stack
            rather than pushing the commit button off-screen. <code>--split</code> puts a destructive
            action where it cannot be hit by aiming for <strong>Save</strong>; <code>--start</code> aligns
            the row to the leading edge, for a form embedded in a page rather than closing a dialog.
            A form longer than the screen keeps the row in reach with <code>--sticky</code> or
            <code>--save-bar</code>, on <a href="/form-saving">Saving and leaving</a>.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Boxes">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.BoxedSettings" Title="Boxed sections" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>.form-section--box</code> frames a section and sets its legend into the frame &mdash; a
            group box, for a page of settings where each part is read on its own. Any field goes inside,
            a <a href="/form-check-list">check list</a> as well as a select. Boxes sit side by side in a
            <a href="/grid"><code>.sedna-grid</code></a>, and the padding is the card's, so they tighten
            with compact density.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.BoxedLegend" Title="Legend contents" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            The legend is a row: an icon, the title and a <code>.badge</code> on the frame's line.
            <code>.form-section-action</code> puts a link, or a small button inside it, on the same line at
            the far end &mdash; an action on the whole section rather than on one field.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.BoxedCheckable" Title="Switched boxes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            A <code>.switch</code> or <code>.form-check</code> in the legend, and
            <code>.choice-dependent</code> with <code>--dim</code> or <code>--hide</code> on the body: the
            body follows the control as a <a href="/form-choice">dependent block</a> follows the option
            above it. A dimmed body is inert, and its values still submit with the form.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.BoxedFolding" Title="Folding boxes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            A <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> with <code>.form-section--box</code>, and the title in a
            <code>.form-section-legend</code> inside its <code>&lt;summary&gt;</code>. A
            <code>.form-section-summary</code> says what is inside while the box is closed, so a reader
            does not open it to find the settings unchanged.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.BoxedStates" Title="Error and disabled boxes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            A <code>.form-error</code> straight under the legend turns the frame, for a rule about the
            section as a whole &mdash; at least one day, at least one agent. <code>disabled</code> on the
            fieldset disables every control in it, which the browser does without a script.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.BoxedFilled" Title="Filled and nested boxes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>--filled</code> tints the inside, for a box that has to stand out from the page around
            it. A box nests in another's body for a part of a part.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Whole forms">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.WholeForm" Title="Complete form" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>Every piece on this page at once, as a starting point to paste and cut down.</p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.SettingsCard" Title="Settings card" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>Fields in a card, actions in a footer row.</p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>
